It seems to me that a lot of people have taken up the habit of writing
pretty stories about their characters when they ascend. Since my nao
characters don't tend to ascend, I've ended up  throwing away rather
large pieces of literature. Perhaps I should post bits as I go along?
Well, here's the first bit, if people like to read this stuff let me
know and I'll post more of it as I go along.

People can watch me play at nethack.alt.org (my nickname is racoontje).

------------

I started off with some rather uninteresting scrolls, the ability to
create monsters somewhat at will (I failed more often than not), an
inadequate potion that would have made me a better person in every way
if it would have been improved a bit, a potion that could make me feel
like I was flying (homebrewn moonshine) and a potion that could have
made me fly for real. The rings I was carrying were more for decoration
than practical use, really. I could pick what to turn into if I'd
undergo a freakish metamorphosis, which I didn't want to (nor could
do). The other ring provided protection from shape changers. They're a
nuisance, but so is starvation. I did, however, bring a crayola I stole
from my three-year-old son.

I started off well by getting totally drunk off of my potion of booze.
Once I was totally wasted, I read reading random scrolls for a quick
laugh. The first one didn't contain any jokes in the first few lines It
could have been my homemade brew but I could have sworn my cloak glowed
purple for a moment there... While drunk, I killed my kitten, and heard
thunder outside of the dungeon. Man, am I glad I was inside and not out
there -- as Rincewind once eloquently put it, "Let's just say that if
complete and utter chaos was lightning, then he'd be the sort to stand
on a hilltop in a thunderstorm wearing wet copper armour and shouting
'All gods are bastards!'". The colorful magicfest didn't stop there --
a scroll made my staff glow blue and I swear I wasn't even tipsy then.
I'm scared of flying so I dipped my potion of levitation in the local
fountain a few times to avoid any accidents.
I found the entrance to a cave or something. Had lots of little gnomes
and ugly dwarves. They weren't very friendly, but neither was I -- they
line up nicely and ate painful
force bolt death. One of the dwarves had a pickaxe, which I now called
my own. Through the dungeon I found some leftover scrolls, none of
which I already knew what they were. The local wererat ate force bolt
death about as quickly as the other inhabitants did. As I saw more and
more pickaxes, gems and other mining equipment, it slowly dawned on me
that I wasn't simply in a well-cultivated cave -- I was in a real mine.
Most likely either the dwarves or the gnomes own it, since they seem to
be the most common inhabitants.
Some of them really hurt me pretty badly, including the rabit rat that
wererat summoned. I hid in the non-existant shadows and sat in a corner
of the well-lit mine. The random dungeon gnome or hobbit too, (you
guessed it) ate force bolt death. Really cool spell - one of the few
that I managed to learn outside of the dungeon. The other spell I know,
Create Monster, is more of an accident than a controlled monster
creation. It's magics own way of darwinism, I guess.

I went down the stairs, only to be greeted by yet more gnomes. You'd
think they'd leave me alone after the umpteenth casualty. I was very
lucky that the owners had installed lighting everywhere -- I was able
to scoot down to levels rather quickly by running past the sluggish
uglies.

I kept dashing down until I ended up in a square room with no doors. It
seemed to be built, rather than carved out of massive rock. I touched
all of the rocks in the wall and low and behold - a secret door. I
figured some people probably lived here, so I reminded myself not to
destroy any property here. Slightly down the hallway that lead to the
newly found door, I found a few other hallways and another door. Again
carefully opening it, I saw a larger room with walls that later turned
out to the edges of shops. A quick chat with the locals (some show pony
with a fancy silver saber) revealed I was in Minetown, GM. Had a quick
look around, but unfortunately I wasn't able to enter one of the shops
yet. Those shopkeepers tend to be rather itchy about the pickaxe I
called my own. I also found a temple dedicated to my diety of choice,
so I hoped I would be able to use my only non-offensive spell to get
some shiny new toys. I plan on stealing most of the priest's money by
convincing him I have none at all. Then, I'd give him all his money
back and get some reward in the process -- I'll just feed him some
bullshit story about the two gold pieces he gave me was the capital I
needed to get started in the exciting market of cursed thorougly rusty
-4 orcish dagger trading.

After having decided to scour Minetown for some more victims for my
gods BBQ pit and after successfully not acquiring any corpses that were
even vaguely usable (I've never hated lycanthropes quite as much as I
do now), I was faced with a gnome. Yay! Proper sacrificing meat. Good
honest gnomes - good food, good sacrificing meat, good carnivore
distraction tool. Heck, I was in the small corrdor betwee two shops and
he had a watchman on the other side of him. I prepared my quarterstaff
for a proper whacking and charged.

Until he read a scroll.

Being in a small corridor was not a good thing at the time. I nearly
developed claustrophobia. The entire corridor was filled with monsters,
and so was the larger space on the town square. In fact, yet another
lycanthrope was breathing down my neck. Sure, they were nicely lined up
for a quick zap of the old force bolt, but there would be new ones to
fille up the ranks seconds afterwards. Plus, I'm pretty sure the guards
wouldn't appreciate one of their colleagues getting their asses kicked.

What did I do? Find out in the next post :-)


